WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:04.770
Nicole: The effects on business that
Covid19 has had, and the shift to

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:08.999
remote working has had a major impact
on the world of cyber security.

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:14.650
Remote working has for many involved
unplanned cloud migrations and the

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:16.849
purchase of new IT products and systems.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:22.220
To get up to speed quickly companies have
bought off the shelf security systems

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:27.190
or have relied on a workers own security
system in their own homes which may

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:29.500
not be adequate or configured properly.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:33.260
This has created new levels
of vulnerability and risk

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:34.920
across a range of industries.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.140
Abraham: The success of remote
working has also meant that there

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:41.839
is now a shift towards making this
a more permanent feature for many.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:45.440
Organisations will therefore need
to assess their current security

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:49.319
infrastructures and address any areas
of weakness that may have appeared

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:53.240
by developing a long-term remote
security strategy, going forward.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:57.110
Ransomware is one of the most common
threats to an organisations cyber security

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:58.882
and this is increasing all the time.

00:00:58.882 --> 00:01:02.910
The techniques used by criminals
are getting more sophisticated and

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:05.327
extortion attacks are on the increase.

00:01:05.327 --> 00:01:09.540
This has a major economic impact
on organisations from not only the

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:13.650
cost of paying the ransom but also
the loss of revenue during any

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:17.490
disruption and the subsequent loss
of business and brand reputation.

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:22.130
Nicole: Traditionally in the past
companies were able to create

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:25.830
secure networks in their office
with a firewall perimeter around it.

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:29.819
This allowed them to then trust
access from that network to

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:31.570
sensitive corporate networks.

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:35.680
With most of the workforce now
working from home, this poses a

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:39.860
new challenge of how to ensure
that only authorised employees are

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:42.210
accessing potentially sensitive data.

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:48.449
Many IT departments rely on VPNs to access
their networks, but these are proving

00:01:48.459 --> 00:01:53.300
inadequate against the threat of phishing
as cyber attacks are on the increase.

00:01:54.190 --> 00:02:00.560
Zero-trust network access or ZTNA is a
new more secure option for controlling

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:05.140
remote access to sensitive data,
reducing the likelihood of an attack.

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:10.690
This involves using a default approach
of “trust nothing” and only allowing

00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:15.410
access using several contextual
authentication metrics, such as the

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:19.230
device that’s being used to connect,
the time they’re connecting, the

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:23.170
location they’re connecting from,
and the behaviour of the user.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:29.880
Abraham: Multi-factor authentication
or MFA can offer an additional

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:33.100
defence against data breaches
and malicious attacks, forcing

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:37.580
individuals to use two or more separate
devices to confirm their identity.

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:42.429
This often involves a one-time passcode
being sent to two or more devices.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:46.859
This can also take the form of a physical
USB device that a person carries with

00:02:46.859 --> 00:02:50.979
them [much like their actual house keys]
which they plug in to their computer to

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:52.750
prove that they are who they claim to be.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:56.620
This works on the fact that whilst
it’s somewhat likely at any given

00:02:56.620 --> 00:03:00.269
time that a criminal may have taken
your credentials, it’s extremely

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:03.850
unlikely that they will have both your
credentials and your second factor,

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:06.179
such as your phone or your USB key.

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:10.560
Nicole: I am sure we all are getting
used to having one-time passcodes

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:14.280
sent to our phones that we must
input into the verification process.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:19.640
However, Microsoft has recently
urged users to move away from

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:24.290
phone-based MFA due to the weak
security in telephone networks and

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:28.760
the fact that SMS messages are not
encrypted giving access to attackers.

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:32.950
Companies should use more
secure MFA methods like Google

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:34.619
or Microsoft authenticators.

00:03:34.759 --> 00:03:40.399
AI and machine learning are growing
in sophistication and capabilities.

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:46.569
AI is being used increasingly to build
automated security systems analysing large

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:49.359
quantities of data at a much faster pace.

00:03:49.969 --> 00:03:53.519
This can offer an opportunity
for greater threat detection, but

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:57.390
this technology can also work both
ways, offering cyber criminals the

00:03:57.390 --> 00:03:59.770
opportunity to automate their attacks.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:04.440
Criminals are then able to take large
data sets of emails and passwords

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:09.330
found on the ‘dark web’ from previous
breaches, and then automatically run

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:13.130
these through all the websites they
want to try to hack, with the hope

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:17.380
that people use the same password and
email for several different sites.

00:04:18.149 --> 00:04:22.079
Once one logs in successfully,
they can then change that password

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:23.810
and take over that account.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:29.219
Abraham: Remote working has increased
the demand on cloud services however

00:04:29.299 --> 00:04:31.290
they are still a prime target for attack.

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:34.770
Organisations need to be on top of
their game when it comes to checking

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:38.320
for vulnerabilities in their current
infrastructure, such as checking

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:40.089
for misconfigured cloud settings.

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:44.089
There have been a growing number of
high-profile cyber attacks on data

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:48.520
privacy and personally identifiable
information or PII records.

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:52.360
Data privacy is now becoming
more of an issue for all of us.

